# Appropriation Bill Debate, 30 April 1960
Tamil title: நிதி ஒதுக்கீட்டுச் சட்ட முன்வடிவு

**Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Mr. Speaker, at the final stage of the debate on this year's finances, the rulers possess the numerical strength to obtain the money they request. The views I express will not prevent them from receiving it. The leader of the Government party should therefore have asked members of the opposition to explain fully and clearly what they think. At least an opportunity should have been given to do so. If people are denied a proper opportunity to voice in the Legislature the thoughts that arise in their minds, the way is opened for them to keep discussing those thoughts outside from many different angles.

Today, however, another duty has intervened for the Finance Minister, compelling us to express our views very briefly. I therefore consider it unnecessary to speak at length about every department. At this moment it is most appropriate to refer only to the conduct of the Cabinet, the nature of the administration, and the standing of the promises Ministers make.

The replies this Cabinet gives to views expressed by the opposition do not appear responsible to me. For example, a member of this House asked about a statement made by a Member of Parliament: was iron taken from Salem and used to build bridges in England?

Forgetting both his own responsibility and the duty and standing of a Member of Parliament, a Minister replied mockingly: “It may have been another Salem. There is even a Salem in America. We have no such information. There is no evidence that iron taken from this Salem was used to build a bridge in England.”

Instead of speaking mockingly, had he later investigated whether evidence existed and where it could be found, then told the House that his earlier remark was wrong and asked the opposition not to feel hurt, I would have praised his generosity. Instead, the Minister spoke in this House in a manner calculated to give newspapers extensive material for portraying the opposition as irresponsible.

I wish to emphasise that this conduct is highly condemnable, that what the Member of Parliament said was entirely relevant, and that evidence exists for it. A book whose title is damaged in the OCR explains with proper evidence where and how iron was taken from Salem and how it was used for two bridges in England.

In countries that seek the healthy growth of democracy, the views expressed by the opposition—even if some are mistaken—must be considered with goodwill. Only by allowing those views to develop will democracy grow. Mocking, ridiculing or rebuking them out of confidence in numerical strength, or telling people not to speak, does not truly develop democracy.

On this issue alone, we could make the public laugh at the entire ruling party. The Minister mocked the claim and said, “Salem iron? It may not be this Salem; there is a Salem even in America.” But we can show the public the evidence that iron from our Salem was indeed taken to England and used to build two bridges.

The Minister spoke without the slightest sense of responsibility, without considering what he was saying or how it would wound others. The Cabinet must reflect on this.

I request the Cabinet not to treat opposition views as deserving such contempt, but to regard them seriously and answer responsibly. It need not show respect to me; those who should show me respect already do. I mention this because democrats outside should be able to respect this Cabinet.

After eleven-thirty, the hon. Finance Minister is to speak with a political or administrative leader arriving from England. He should consider how that visitor would ridicule them if he knew how this Cabinet conducts itself.

Secondly, those on our side argued that the rulers' policies are defective. When I say “our side,” the opposition consists of two or three different groups. Whenever the Cabinet has the opportunity, it cleverly uses our divisions and differences to frame its replies. I regret to say that it does not give direct explanations or satisfactory answers to the actual views each group expresses.

This Cabinet must change its basic belief that everything done in this country must be financed by imposing a tax. It must also create ways for the Cabinet and ruling party to earn revenue. Only then, in a growing economy and at a time when the people's needs continue to increase, can they provide the facilities the country requires.

With this outlook, I have repeatedly urged the rulers to seek many revenue-producing opportunities. Even if their reply is merely, “Not now; we will do it gradually,” let them at least accept this as policy, quickly seek sources of revenue, and offer some relief to a suffering people.

Whether by pressing the Central Government or using the extraordinary abilities they are said to possess, I ask them to make every effort to establish an atomic power station here. Our available hydroelectric opportunities have been exhausted, yet electricity is essential for developing the industries this State needs.

Likewise, I ask them to combine the project to convert Tuticorin into a deep-water port with the Sethusamudram project and arrange for their early completion.

If our industrial resources are to expand and yield good results, rail transport in the South must be developed to the necessary extent…

[The remainder of this transport passage in Image 4 is damaged.]

When this was raised, Finance Minister Subramaniam responded to Praja Socialist Party leader Thiru Chinnadurai with considerable anger: “You may attend to everything when you come to power. This is the limit of our ability.” He spoke as though overcome by humility. That may be regarded as theatrical humility. But when we consider how strongly they actually press our demands before the Central Government, their conduct in this House disappoints the public.

Fortunately, our Public Works Minister is present. I have raised the Palar question in this House ten times, not once. Each time, the Minister rose and gave a hope-inspiring answer: “We are arguing the matter. We have convened a conference. We have spoken with the Mysore Government.”

Recently, however, answering a question in Parliament, a Central Minister said: “The Madras administration stated that it did not wish to pursue the matter further. We have therefore closed the question.” Why give this House words that mislead us? If you cannot fight today, openly admit your inability. Instead, you say, “We are speaking with the Central Government.” While you speak like this, Ministers in Parliament reply from the Centre in a way that blackens the face of this Government—as though slapping it across the cheek. They say they remain inactive because the Madras administration said nothing further need be done.

I ask this Cabinet: is this not shameful? Consider the replies you give us in this House and your conduct.

**The Hon. P. Kakkan:** The respected hon. member asks whether we have no shame. That is improper. We are conducting negotiations with the Central Government on this matter.

**Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** I pity our Minister if this is all the answer he can give. My point is that Ministers of the Government of India speak in Parliament in a way that makes the Cabinet's answer here deserving of ridicule and condemnation. Either these Ministers have deceived us or the Central Government has deceived them. Whichever it is, it is shameful and condemnable.

You have brought the people to a boiling point. I tell the Ministers with certainty that you will lose votes because of this. I have pointed out the duty owed to the people and spoken about it in this House ten times. I will not speak about it here again. It would not be difficult to rouse the people and obtain it through a picket before the Minister's house.

They habitually speak irresponsibly, in the manner of a schoolchild.

**The Hon. P. Kakkan:** I have not answered irresponsibly in this House. If the matter had to be considered, I said we would do so immediately. It is not surprising that this was turned to advantage and reported to the people. I repeat that they are now conducting talks. If Ministers in Parliament speak mockingly, what can one say on seeing it?

[The remainder of the exchange at the end of Image 6 is damaged.]

It was said here that we do not want milestones written in Hindi and that we have written to the Central Government about it. But the Minister there says he has received no such communication from the Madras administration.

**The Hon. C. Subramaniam:** I spoke only about Hindi numerals. I said there was no need to introduce Hindi numerals, not merely here but throughout India.

**Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Here you speak against the milestones; outside you condemn them and try to win the people's affection. Thus either you want to deceive the people here or you fear the people there. To use the Finance Minister's own idiom for these two accusations, we must ask whether this cap fits this head. I wish only to point out that deficiencies exist.

The Minister says with pride that they have asked the Central Government to separate our Tamil region from the food zone and make Madras State a separate unit, for only then will the food problem be solved. Kindly consider the contradiction between the answers given here and those given there. Does the Cabinet lose respect there, or does it lose respect here? I wish to stress that a loss of respect in either place is not good.

Finally, when the land-ceiling bill was debated, examination showed that not much land would become available to poor people. I therefore ask you to bring forward quickly a sound scheme to take land that is not now cultivated, give it to cultivators, and convert it into productive farmland.

As our Finance Minister said yesterday, bring unemployed people—human beings with human power—directly under the administration's control. In English this is called a “land army.” Establish such a labour force for industrial development and another for agricultural development, and increase our resources.

You are certainly going to obtain the sum you have requested. Receive it without anger. Receive it while taking an interest in hearing what the opposition says. With that, I conclude my speech.

---NOTES---
The scan contains eight continuous pages and a complete conclusion. The book title cited as evidence for Salem iron is irrecoverable from its mixed-script OCR and is not invented in translation. The closing portion of Image 4 concerning southern rail transport and the end of Image 6 are damaged and explicitly omitted. “Land army” is retained from the source's own English gloss; it denotes a publicly organised workforce for land development, not a military force. The Tamil source remains verbatim.
